hi everyone my name is nathra srinivasan  and my research project is the effect of  the onset of menopause on the  transcriptomic profile of the aging  female brain  there appears to be a divergence in the  occurrence of neurodegenerative diseases  between  males and females but the mechanisms so  far are unknown  for example for alzheimer's disease more  than 5.5 million americans are living  with alzheimer's disease  of whom two-thirds are elderly females  in addition the mitochondria of younger  females are protected against  the pathologies of alzheimer's disease  such as beta-amyloid toxicity reactive  oxygen species and apoptogenic signals  compared to their male counterparts  however this advantage  is lost in the mitochondria of elderly  females  and the difference between the younger  females and the elderly females  is this transition into menopause  and in addition current alzheimer's  treatments only target acetylcholine and  glutamate  um neurotransmitters in order to treat  the symptoms  but they are not treating the root cause  and so because of this discrepancy  between the two genders  and this transitioning into menopause  it's important  to figure out the underlying causes to  be able to better target treatments  so i will be um figuring out what is  going on as a female ages and if there  are any transcriptomic differences to  identify the genes at play  as well as pinpoint pathways to better  target treatments  first of all to give some background  information about female aging menopause  is the end of a female's menstrual cycle  and fertility  in which the ovaries no longer produce  estrogen and progesterone  in this project i will be looking at  data obtained from  rats and rat menopause so the ages that  i will be talking  about here are in rats age  so the first stage is pre-menopause  which is about six months for  rats and this is when they have normal  menstrual cycles  then they have transition into  perimenopause at around nine months  in which the cycles are irregular but  they have not stopped completely  then they transition into menopause at  about nine months  which starts with the final menstrual  cycle  and then at around 15 months they will  be at in post menopause which  in humans this is the year mark  from the final cycle now  to continue on about a little bit of  background about the aging of the female  brain  as the brain ages it's shown that there  is an increased immune response  and this is obtained from mishra el from  which  uh i am using the data from  so for the data set distribution um i'm  using transcriptomic data  from the rat female hippocampus  and there are six groups with six  samples each so there is the  female rat at six months or reg six  m and then at nine months normal  menstrual cycle or reg nine  m then nine months irregular menstrual  cycle which is erect nine m  uh nine months acyclic menstrual cycle  acyclic  9m then ovx at nine months which is  overrectimized and overrectimize  basically models the most  important clinical features of estrogen  deficiency induced or  post-menopausal conditions and this way  it acts as a control for my  experiment and then there's also uh the  female rat  at 16 months acyclic menstrual cycle or  acyclic  16m so my research  question and hype for my research  question and hypothesis  i'd like to understand the genes  expressed um differently in the  progressive stages of female  aging and menopause which can help us  understand the risks for  neurodegeneration and pathways to target  in order to treat near degeneration such  as  for alzheimer's disease in women so  the different ideas that i'm looking at  as how is the age and time of the onset  of menopause affecting the transcriptome  expression and which pathways are  affected and are the bar  are there biomarkers and menopausal  women that can help us identify their  risk for certain neurodegenerative  disorders or how does the expression  differ in overectomized  specimens and regular specimens and can  this lead to insights on the onset of  neurodegenerative disease  and my central question is what are the  effects of the onset of menopause on the  transcriptomic profile of the aging  female brain  so i hypothesize that as the females  enter premenopause and peri-menopause  they'll  express less genes related to  neurodegeneration as  the females and menopause and  post-menopause  and basically uh that the age and the  age of the onset do have an effect  on the transcriptomics of the rat  so for my methodology um since there are  six groups to compare i'll be using  principal component analysis  um dc2 which is differential gene  expression analysis and hierarchical  clustering  in order to relate the expression to  each other and draw conclusions  analyze what genes are overexpressed and  underexpressed and the six groups and  look at the significantly expressed  genes to annotate their function and  david and richard to see the pathways  that are expressed  so firstly here um with my preliminary  pca i found that there appeared to be  a divergence between the regular  groups and the irregular and acyclic  groups  and therefore i uh grouped it into  regular versus acyclic and regular  versus irregular  and as we can see in the pca they're two  different clusters for regular versus  irregular  um which shows that they're different  and for the regular versus  irregular um there does not appear to be  two  separate clusters but that's only  because um the components  are very small but once i um did  differential expression and i  did the pca again for the um separate  uh significant genes i found that there  appeared to be  two distinct clusters and also to test  the control  um i did pca between the regular versus  ovx and i found that there were two  different clusters  showing that there appears to be a  divergence when there's the shift into  menopause  and it cancels out for the age  and uh continuing on from my  differential expression  um i obtained some plots such as these  volcano plots which shows the  significant  genes um and i also found that  the pathways that are upregulated in  both the regular versus irregular and  regular versus acyclic were um functions  related to  the immune system and the pathways that  were down regulated were related to  mitochondrial dysfunctioning um such as  oxidative phosphorylation and the  mitochondrial respiratory chain complex  which in turn leads to the down  regulation of the pathways for  neurodegeneration such as  alzheimer's parkinson's and huntington's  and for further hierarchical clustering  i found that there appeared to be two  different clusters  um which shows that there is a  discrepancy between the regular versus  acyclic and the regular versus irregular  and i did pathway analysis with the  functional annotation  and i have images over here um  showing the relative expression of the  upregulated and down regulated pathways  so here's  upregulated immune system um  downregulated related to the  mitochondrial function  and near degenerative diseases same  thing over here for regular versus  acyclic and so for my conclusion  and discussion i found that there is an  impact on the transcriptomics of the rat  especially in relation to the  mitochondrial function leading to  neurodegenerative diseases such as  alzheimer's and parkinson's and  huntington's  i also found that the immune system is  heightened  um through this data and this helps me  say that um the pathways that should be  targeted for possible treatments of near  degenerative diseases  should be um in relation to oxidative  stress  um and this also supports the current  pathologies for alzheimer's in which  um the accumulation of the beta amyloid  um plaques exacerbates the oxidative  stress which damages the mitochondrial  structure  which shows that through targeting  menopause we can possibly be able to  find  certain treatments because menopause  appears to be a factor  in the onset of near-degenerative  diseases now for my limitations i was  not able to extract this data myself in  a lab and i had to use the data that  other researchers extracted from their  samples  so i was not able to control the sample  size or the procedures used to collect  the data and therefore i'm dependent on  the collection strategy  to obtain my results and  uh for the implications um by  now that we uh through my project we can  see that menopause is a factor on  the oxidative stress which leads to  these neurodegenerative diseases  um by being able to remove the oxidative  the cause of the oxidative stress um  according to coal  at all um with the oxidative stress  it creates damage on the mitochondria  but once the cause of the oxidative  stress  is removed the mitochondrial membrane is  actually able to  rebound and maintain  its structure again which could possibly  help us  with um targeting for neurodegenerative  diseases  and could be revolutionary to the field  so thank you for listening to my  presentation
 

